WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:14.630
You may have noticed that, lately, we’ve
been talking a lot about Argentina here on

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:15.900
Visualpolitik.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.610
And you’re right, we’ve made a lot of
videos about the country of tango.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.790
But, what do you want me to say?

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.540
Argentina is a very interesting country.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.630
After all, can you tell me of any other country
that in the last 6 decades, on average, has

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.349
had to negotiate a rescue with the IMF every
4 and a half years?

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:33.349
Because they thought they could do anything.

00:00:33.349 --> 00:00:34.349
And ended up having to revise their decisions.)

00:00:34.349 --> 00:00:35.349
Argentina has almost become synonymous for
crisis...

00:00:35.349 --> 00:00:37.320
What the hell, Argentina is the country of
crises.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:45.180
Monday 08/12/2019: Argentina just suffered
the 2nd-biggest crash since 1950 for any stock

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.260
market — and the nation is again on the
brink of a financial crisis.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.260
Business Insider.)

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.370
Everything is head over heels.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:56.210
Companies dropped between 30 and 50%, the
country’s risk rose by 350 points in one

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.650
hour and the dollar rose, a lot.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.440
All this shows that there’s a serious problem.)

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:08.200
I give you my word, I can’t remember a single
time when Argentina’s current affairs weren’t

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.480
marked by economic problems.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:18.170
The hyperinflation of the late ‘80s, the
default and the corralito, or freezing of

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.220
bank accounts, at the beginning of this century
or the economic collapse that the country

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:28.110
has been experiencing since 2015 and which
has forced it to negotiate with the IMF, yet

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.110
again.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.730
On this occasion, the rescue amounts to no
less than 56 billion dollars.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.510
Yes, you did hear that correctly, 56 billion
dollars.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.230
Obviously having so many economic problems
has consequences.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:44.230
During the last 100 years, Argentina has gone
from being one of the 10 richest countries

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:48.970
on the planet in per capita terms to tumbling
way down to 64th position.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:53.460
That is, at the level of Belarus, Turkmenistan
or Iran.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.500
And worst of all, it’s ranking keeps falling.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.390
But in recent years a dream has spread through
Argentina, and with it the hope that, once

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.380
and for all, they’ll be able to leave their
problems behind.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.460
In the nineteenth century, wool was Argentina’s
cash cow, at the beginning of the last century,

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:15.560
it was beef, the famous Argentine meat, in
the mid-twentieth century, it was wheat and

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.590
now many hope that oil and gas will change
the country’s fate.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.430
And this hope has a name: Dead Cow.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:23.430
Listen up.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:29.170
What investments did you bring if the investments
you’re making are being made in Dead Cow?

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.170
Incredible.)

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.170
(DEAD COW, THE LATEST TREASURE)

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.500
Ok, what can we do, it doesn’t have the
prettiest or most romantic name, but behind

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.400
this name lies one of Argentina’s great
treasures.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.850
But... hold on a second.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:45.710
What on earth is this Dead Cow deal?

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:49.890
That’s the obvious first question, don't
you think?

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:54.990
Dead Cow, is a geological formation that can
turn this country into a gas exporting power.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.060
Located on the western edge of Patagonia,
near the Andes and in the middle of an arid

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.950
and lonely steppe, Dead Cow is the reason
for news stories like this:

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.330
Dead Cow.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:08.480
June's gas production was the highest in 11
years.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.300
Last month there was a total of 140 million
cubic meters per day, which meant an annual

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.300
growth of 5.8%.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.180
It’s the largest gas production in eleven
years.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.150
La Nación)

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.400
By the way, this isn’t strictly a new discovery.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.720
The Dead Cow site was discovered by the American
geologist Charles Weaver back in 1931.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.300
However, until relatively recently nothing
could be done with it.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.230
Dead Cow, is an unconventional hydrocarbon
deposit, that is, “Shale oil” when oil

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:42.840
is extracted and “Shale gas” when gas
is extracted.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.660
In this site, hydrocarbons are distributed
throughout a 30,000 km2 surface… an area

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.930
equivalent to twice the size of Kuwait.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.080
There’s only one problem, unlike the Middle
East where you only need to prick the soil

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:01.040
to get oil or gas to spout, here, in Dead
Cow territory, all these precious hydrocarbon

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.040
resources are about 3,000 meters below the
surface.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.489
Yes, very tricky

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:12.010
And wait a second, because that’s not all.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:16.120
There’s another, even worse problem.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.530
In this case, oil and gas are trapped within
the rocks, so to exploit them, a well must

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:25.310
be built, through which a pressurised substance
is injected to fracture the rocks so that

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:28.030
the oil and gas can be released through that
same well.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.729
It’s a matter of pressure, when oil and
gas are released from the rock these compounds

00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:34.360
emerge with a huge force.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.059
It’s something similar to opening a shaken
bottle of Coke or soda.

00:04:39.059 --> 00:04:44.240
Exactly, we’re talking about the controversial
fracking or hydraulic fracture.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.669
That’s why they couldn’t exploit Dead
Cow until relatively recently.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:51.629
Now, at this point, there’s a question I
know many of you may be thinking.

00:04:51.629 --> 00:04:55.030
Exactly how much oil and how much gas are
we talking about?

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.620
Because that’s the key issue.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.409
Could the Dead Cow site really represent a
turnaround for Argentina?

00:05:01.409 --> 00:05:02.409
Listen up

00:05:02.409 --> 00:05:07.080
Thanks to Dead Cow we produced 3% more gas
than a year ago.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.360
We hadn’t had such a high production since
2011.]

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.740
(OXYGEN AGAINST THE CRISIS)

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:18.509
To continue this story, we need to go back
to 2011.

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:23.560
That year, Repsol, a Spanish oil company,
announced “its greatest discovery”:

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.460
Repsol YPF announces the greatest oil discovery
in its history.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.990
Repsol YPF estimates that there are recoverable
oil and gas resources equivalent to 927 million

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:33.990
barrels of oil.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:34.990
El País (7/11/2011))

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.110
But that was only the beginning.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:41.889
Today, Dead Cow is considered to be at least
the second largest unconventional gas reservoir

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:44.479
in the world and the fourth largest in shale
oil.

00:05:44.479 --> 00:05:49.159
In fact, check it out, according to the US
Energy Agency estimates, this deposit’s

00:05:49.159 --> 00:05:55.529
current exploitable reserves amount to 16
billion barrels of oil and 308 trillion cubic

00:05:55.529 --> 00:05:57.199
feet of natural gas.

00:05:57.199 --> 00:05:58.199
But...

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:01.140
What do these figures really equate to?

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.960
Well, to give you an idea...

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.409
16 billion barrels of oil equals twice the
conventional oil reserves of countries like

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:12.719
Norway or Angola.

00:06:12.719 --> 00:06:19.470
It’s almost 60 years of Argentina’s oil
consumption.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.509
And as for natural gas...

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:25.199
Argentina’s current consumption is approximately
1 trillion cubic feet per year.

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:30.340
That is, to understand this better, just the
exploitable reserves of Dead Cow, 308 trillion

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:38.749
cubic feet of natural gas, amount to almost
300 years of gas consumption in Argentina.

00:06:38.749 --> 00:06:44.259
Dead Cow might as well be called “cash cow”,
because the place is so flush with oil and

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:49.409
gas.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:54.090
All this means that within a few years Argentina
could have an important competitive advantage:

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:55.099
cheap energy.

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:58.530
And nothing suits industry better than having
cheap energy.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:03.089
On the other hand, the country may also delve
into exporting natural gas, not only to its

00:07:03.089 --> 00:07:07.710
Latin American neighbors, but to the rest
of the world, especially Asia.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:13.939
And that’s interesting, because surely most
of you are worried about sustainability and

00:07:13.939 --> 00:07:15.229
the environment, right?

00:07:15.229 --> 00:07:20.270
Who wouldn’t like to have renewable short-term
energy sources, and for lower polluting energy

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.699
sources to have a leading role?

00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:25.960
However, to reach this point there’s a much
more urgent step that needs to be taken: replacing

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:30.189
coal in production processes, especially in
developing countries, such as China, which

00:07:30.189 --> 00:07:35.819
still generates 70% of its electricity by
burning coal.

00:07:35.819 --> 00:07:43.339
With its enormous population and heavy reliance
on coal, China is by far the world’s biggest

00:07:43.339 --> 00:07:46.740
polluter, responsible for more emissions than
the US and EU combined.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:47.740
CarbonBrief)

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:51.909
And in this mission, both for cost and technology,
natural gas works like a charm and is certainly

00:07:51.909 --> 00:07:54.080
much cleaner than oil and coal.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.550
So even if we speak in terms of sustainability,
“Dead Cow” is great news.

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:58.550
But just a second, at this point some of you
may be thinking, this could be good for the

00:07:58.550 --> 00:07:59.550
environment and in several years all this
may help Argentina.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:00.550
Well you couldn't be more wrong.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:11.229
Dead Cow is already having very positive consequences
for the Argentine economy.

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:15.550
You see, the lack of investment and the decline
of old deposits have caused Argentina, since

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:20.379
2008, to import a huge amount of natural gas
and oil, which of course meant a much higher

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:24.289
trade balance deficit and a huge exodus of
dollars from the country.

00:08:24.289 --> 00:08:26.909
However, now that’s beginning to change.

00:08:26.909 --> 00:08:32.469
“The production of crude oil accumulates
16 months of uninterrupted annual growth.

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:38.969
The unconventional oil, which increased by
47%, already represents 18% of the total production.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:42.320
Argentina hasn’t had to resort to oil imports
for 14 months”.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.330
Gustavo Lopetegui, Secretary of Energy.)

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.940
And with natural gas, it’s a similar story.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.880
Since 2017, net imports have decreased by
more than 50%.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:51.880
“Our great challenge lies in continuing
to increase production to supply the domestic

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:52.880
market and to become gas exporters in all
months of the year.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.070
This will allow us to converge at lower prices
in the domestic market, both for homes and

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:55.070
the industrial sector”.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.070
Gustavo Lopetegui, Secretary of Energy.)

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:59.890
Obviously, without this change, Argentina’s
ongoing crisis would’ve been even worse,

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:00.890
much worse.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:04.890
What’s more, if Mauricio Macri were to get
re-elected, the first thing he should do is

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.770
go kiss the earth at Dead Cow, don't you think?

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:12.410
The point is that if this site is put to work
at full capacity, the impact on the country's

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:16.930
foreign balance could be huge.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:21.180
According to Carlos Ormachea, president of
Tecpetrol, one of the companies that is working

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:26.100
on the site, we would be talking about a positive
impact of 30 billion dollars per year.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.070
20 billion in exports and another 10 billion
dollars which wouldn’t be spent on buying

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.890
hydrocarbons from outside of the country.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:36.820
And of course, the country could really use
aaaall those dollars to overcome its external

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:37.820
imbalances.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:38.820
This is what we need, to shake it off and
notice everything we can do.]

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:39.820
Now do you understand why it’s so important?

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:41.490
From the IMF’s rescue, we’d go into the
Dead Cow’s mega-rescue.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:46.720
But, just a second, because things aren’t
that straightforward.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:51.060
Listen up.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:57.890
We’ve definitely indebted the Argentine
Republic.]

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.290
The highest salary in all of Latin America
with an increase of more than 2,000%.]

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:03.910
(POLITICS, ALWAYS POLITICS)

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:07.690
One question, do you remember the Repsol discovery
from 2011 we mentioned?

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:12.190
Well, that huge news was actually the beginning
of many, many problems.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.910
With so much money involved...

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:19.630
The then Argentinian president, Cristina Fernández
de Kirchner made a controversial decision:

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:24.840
to forcefully nationalize the YPF company,
the largest Argentine oil company, which at

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.020
that time was part of the Repsol group.

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.260
Suddenly, the Argentine government took over…

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:30.390
And everything stopped.

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.970
After its nationalization, they didn’t only
have to deal with the beginning of a very

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:37.760
long judicial process which ended up costing
the country a lot of money, international

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:39.360
investments also stopped completely.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.283
And, the oil and gas sector is one that requires
huge investments of thousands and thousands

00:10:45.283 --> 00:10:46.490
of billions of dollars.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:48.830
In the end the project almost stopped.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.930
However, in 2015, when Mauricio Macri came
to power, the project came to life again and

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:57.940
a huge rain of millions began to exploit this
great treasure.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.340
But a lot more is still needed.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:04.010
Will Argentine politicians allow Dead Cow
to develop?

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:07.280
Will Argentina be able to take advantage of
this great resource?

